<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="matrices-complete">
    <title>Matrices (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Matrices are a foundational tool in linear algebra, and used for representing observables 
                        (like energy, momentum) in quantum systems. We'll establish some necessary basics for working with and
                        describing matrices, and then work towards understanding deeper uses and interpretations of matrices.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Matrix Structure</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
               An <m>m \times n</m> <term>matrix</term> has
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>m</m> rows and <m>n</m> columns, in which each entry holds a scalar (either a real number or 
                        a complex number, depending on context).
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Some special matrices are row vectors (<m>m=1</m>), column vectors (<m>n=1</m>), and square matrices 
                        (<m>m=n</m>).
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        It is often convenient to view matrices as a collection of column vectors, written
                        <md>
                            \left[ \vvec_1 \quad \vvec_2 \cdots \vvec_n \right]
                        </md>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise>
                <title>Matrix Arithmetic</title>
             <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The <em>sum</em> of two matrices, denoted <m>A + B</m>, is the matrix whose
                                                entries are the sums of the corresponding entries of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
                                                Compute <m>A +B</m> for each pair of matrices below, when possible. If not possible, explain why.
                        <ol>
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}1&amp;3&amp;-1 \\ -2&amp;2&amp;1 \\ 7&amp;8&amp;-10\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                            <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;2&amp;-5 \\1&amp;3&amp;-1 \\ -5&amp;2&amp;13\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                        
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;5 \\ -2&amp;3 \\ 10&amp;4\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                            <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 3 \\ 3 &amp; 5 \\ 1 &amp; -1\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>\displaystyle \begin{bmatrix}2&amp;1 \\ 1 &amp; 2\end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                                                </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        See notes from class for solutions.
                    </p>
                </solution>
             </task>

             <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The number <m>0</m> is called an <em>additive identity</em> because
                                                <m>a + 0 = 0 +a = a</m> for any number <m>a</m>.
                                                Is there an additive identity for <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices?
                                                If so, what is it?
                    </p>
                </statement>
             </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       For any number <m>a</m>, the number <m>-a</m> is called an
                                               <em>additive inverse</em> of <m>a</m> because <m>a + (-a) = 0</m>.
                                               Does the matrix
                                               <m>\displaystyle \begin{bmatrix}2&amp;1 \\ 1 &amp; 2\end{bmatrix}</m> have an
                                               additive inverse?
                                               If so, what is it? 
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        See notes from class for solutions.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>b</m> is any real number, the matrix <m>bA</m> is the matrix obtained by
                                                multiplying every entry of <m>A</m> by the number <m>b</m>.
                                                This is called <em>scalar multiplication</em>.
                                                 Compute <m>bA</m> for each pair <m>b,A</m> below, if possible. If not possible,
                                                explain why.
                                                <ol>
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}1&amp;3&amp;-1 \\ -2&amp;2&amp;1 \\ 7&amp;8&amp;-10\end{bmatrix}, b= 3</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                        
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;5 \\ -2&amp;3 \\ 10&amp;4\end{bmatrix}, b = -5</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                                                </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        See notes from class for solutions.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    Does the set of all real matrices form a vector space over <m>\R</m>? Explain why or why not.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        No. This set is not closed under addition. However, the set of all <m>m \times n</m> matrices does form
                        an <m>mn</m>-dimensional vector space.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise>
        <title>Matrix Multiplication</title>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <em>Matrix/Vector:</em>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>\left[\vvec_1 \cdots \vvec_n \right] \cdot \left[\begin{array}{r} c_1 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \end{array}\right] = c_1\vvec_1 + \cdots + c_n\vvec_n</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <em>Matrix/Matrix:</em>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    If <m>A =\left[\vvec_1 \cdots \vvec_n \right] </m> and <m>B = \left[\wvec_1 \cdots \wvec_n \right]</m>, 
                    then <m>A \cdot B = \left[A\wvec_1 \cdots A\wvec_n \right]</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find <m>A \cdot B</m> and <m>B \cdot A</m>, when possible, for each pair of
                                        matrices below.
                                        If the product is not possible, explain why.
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 3 &amp; -1 \\ -2 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\ 7 &amp; 8 &amp; 2\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                    <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 2 &amp; -5 \\1 &amp; 3 &amp; -1 \\ -5 &amp; 2 &amp; -3\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                    
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;5 \\ -2&amp;3 \\ 10&amp;4\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                    <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 3 &amp; 3\\ 3 &amp; 5 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                    
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                    <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}-1&amp;5 \\ 6&amp;10\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    See solutions from class.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Matrix Multiplication (continued)</title>
       <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Is matrix multiplication commutative?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                No! In fact, there are times when one ordering of matrix multiplication is defined and the other ordering is not.
            </p>
        </solution>
       </task>

       <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Zero Divisors:</em>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Unlike with real numbers, it is possible to multiply two non-zero matrices and get the zero matrix.
            </p>
        </solution>
       </task>

       <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Multiplicative Identity:</em>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The multiplicative identity for square matrices <m>I_n</m> is the matrix with <m>1</m> down the main diagonal, and 
                <m>0</m> everywhere else. For an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, it satisfies <m>A\cdot I_n =I_n \cdot A = A </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
       </task>
       
       
        </exercise>
    
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <title>Other Operations on Matrices</title>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <term>Matrix Transpose</term>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        For an <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, <m>A^T</m> is the <m>n \times m</m> matrix whose columns are the 
                        rows of <m>A</m>, and whose rows are the columns of <m>A</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <term>Trace</term> (square matrices)
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>\text{tr}(A)</m> is the sum of the diagonal entries of <m>A</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <term>Inverse</term> (square matrices)
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The inverse matrix <m>A^{-1}</m> of <m>A</m> is the matrix such that <m>A^{-1} \cdot A = A \cdot A^{-1} = I_n</m>.
                        Not every square matrix has an inverse matrix.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <term>Determinant</term> (square matrices)
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The determinant of a matrix <m>A</m> is a scalar whose computation depends on the dimension of <m>A</m>. For 
                        <m>2\times 2</m> matrices it is computed as <m>ad-bc</m>. For matrices of higher dimension, you can use technology to compute it.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Among other things, the determinant tells us when a matrix is invertible: a square matrix 
                        <m>A</m> is invertible exactly when <m>\text{det}(A) \neq 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            
        </exercise>
    </page>


       
    </worksheet>
